  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Trump's May 23 statement that the Iran-US deal is "largely negotiated" and will be "announced soon," followed by a US official's May 24 claim via Reuters/Axios that the MOU is expected signed "in the coming days" and that Khamenei approved the broad framework, and then CBS News May 25 reporting that negotiators reached agreement on broad principles including HEU disposal — the hardest red line in the negotiation — constitute the most substantive convergent chain on the US side since talks began. The proposed 60-day framework — Hormuz reopened with mines cleared, US port-blockade lifted, oil-sale sanctions waivers issued, followed by formal nuclear and territorial negotiations — was described as text being finalized, not conceptually contested. No signed document exists as of May 25, and Iranian principal confirmation (Araghchi, Pezeshkian) remains absent. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>The Knesset dissolution bill's 110-0 preliminary reading on May 20 — with Netanyahu absent and all major coalition factions co-sponsoring — advanced the probability of a formal early-election announcement before June 1 materially above its initial assignment. The bill has not passed a second or third reading, no election date has been set, and Netanyahu's deliberate non-attendance was interpreted by Israeli commentators as a tactical move to preserve side-deal leverage with Haredi partners rather than a commitment to early elections. The resolution bar for <span class="cite">F2</span> (formal announcement naming a date, or passed final-reading dissolution motion) remains unmet. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>

      <p>All May 22-24 Hormuz transit data originates exclusively from IRGC and Iranian state media — the IRGC Navy claimed 33 ships transited on May 24 and 35 ships on May 22 — none from the four qualifying independent sources (Lloyd's List, Windward, USNI News, UNCTAD). Iranian media simultaneously contradicted Trump's framing by insisting the strait remains under Iranian sovereign management and will not revert to pre-war navigation norms. Even accepting IRGC figures at face value, 33 per day is 34% below the ≥50 threshold. Brent crude fell $4.71 on May 25 to $98.83 — markets took the US official's "coming days" statement seriously — though Lloyd's war-risk premiums remain at 2.5–5% of hull, and the insurance market has not moved. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>On May 24, an Israeli soldier (Sgt. Nehoray Leizer, 19) was killed in Bint Jbeil in south Lebanon — the tenth soldier killed during the truce period. This is consistent with the established porous-truce dynamic and does not meet the formal collapse threshold that was being tracked by the now-resolved Fa; the May 29 Pentagon security track and June 2-3 fourth-round political talks in Washington remain scheduled. The Lebanese government condemned Israeli violations but did not declare the truce void. The ceasefire formally holds under its 45-day extension through late June. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:16%;">Escalation 16%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:73%;">Protracted 73%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:11%;">De-esc 11%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (16%):</strong> Deal fails at the principal-confirmation stage — Khamenei disavows HEU disposal or Iran publicly rejects the Axios/CBS framework — collapsing the negotiating track and resuming active hostilities.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (73%):</strong> The modal outcome: either a deal is signed but the multi-step Hormuz recovery sequence (mines, insurance, carriers) is not completed within June 10 measurement window, or no deal is reached and the porous-truce dynamic continues across multiple theaters without formal resolution. Computed: p(deal YES, Hormuz NO) = 0.54×0.80 = 0.43; p(deal NO, no escalation) = 0.46×0.65 = 0.30; total 0.73.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (11%):</strong> Deal signed with Iranian principal authentication before June 5, Hormuz independently confirmed at ≥50 vessels/day by June 10, Lloyd's war-risk premiums decline, and major carriers resume scheduled transit. Computed: p(F3 YES=0.54) × p(F4 YES|F3 YES=0.20) = 0.11.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iran's internal communication pattern on the MOU negotiations divides along a consistent line: the diplomatic apparatus (Araghchi, Ghalibaf) engages actively while semi-official and hardline media (Fars, PressTV) deny or minimize concessions. Pezeshkian met Pakistan's Army Chief Munir on May 23 in Tehran — the Pakistan back-channel remaining active even as the US side described "coming days" for a signature — which the Iranian foreign ministry framed as "preventing escalation," not as brokering a final text. This dual-track communication is the same pattern Iran ran in April and early May: negotiating teams advance while public-facing statements deny proximity. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>
      <p>The specific sticking point reported by multiple May 24 sources is Hormuz sovereignty: Iran's position is that the strait lies within Iranian and Omani territorial waters and will remain under IRGC coordination even after any agreement, with a toll regime (the Persian Gulf Strait Authority, announced May 18) as the permanent framework. Trump's demand for "full freedom of navigation" and Iran's insistence on "sovereign management" are not reconciled in the current draft text. The IRGC's daily vessel-authorization announcements (25 ships May 23, 33 ships May 24) function as a political demonstration that Iran controls access, not as genuine commercial reopening. Western shipping insurers have not altered their positions. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>
      <p>Khamenei's reported approval of the "broad framework" — attributed to a US official via Reuters/Axios, denied by Iran's semi-official Fars channel — is the key factual dispute in the current 24-48 hour window. CBS May 25 adds HEU disposal as an agreed broad principle, which if accurate resolves the deepest structural uncertainty about Iranian decision-making at the principal level. If the reports are inaccurate or premature, the gap between Pezeshkian's negotiating position and Khamenei's approval threshold remains the primary blocker. No Khamenei public statement on the deal had been issued as of May 25; Araghchi has not publicly confirmed any agreed text. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F1</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

